From c13d5013838e3f9f699e2532b51df435810530b1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Che Haijun <chehaijun@Mac.localdomain>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 14:24:51 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] feat(blocks): let the researcher assemble the tool they
 need
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The core is documents, codes and codings. Everything else is a capability block
the researcher switches on or off, and the choice is stored with the project so
which tools a study used becomes part of the record. Switching a block off
removes it from the interface rather than greying it out: the workspace matches
the study instead of the study fitting the workspace.

Six blocks: theme grouping, memos, AI suggestions, frequency analysis, audit
trail, and the plotting workbench. The plotting workbench is listed with a
"planned" tag and cannot be switched on yet, so its absence is visible rather
than silent.

This also corrects a mistake in docs/NOT_BUILDING.md. I had read Pi Agent's
"What we didn't build" as "we will never build this" and cut plotting outright.
Pi's list actually means "not in the core", and every entry has a documented
extension path. The document is rewritten into three tiers — core, optional
blocks, and the short list of things genuinely ruled out on methodological
grounds (automatic coding, cloud accounts, real-time collaboration, semantic
search) — and plotting moves from "not doing" to "an optional block".

Schema v4 adds the block list; migrate() upgrades v3 projects with the default
set, so nothing changes for existing work.
